    D3D11 bridge: release at the game's xrEndFrame, copy and capture together (V440)
    
    A bridged D3D11 release copies the game's image into the runtime's at
    xrReleaseSwapchainImage, and the copy reads what the game's context had
    submitted when it signalled. A game that releases an eye before all of
    its rendering is submitted shows a frame-old image in that eye alone:
    IL-2 Great Battles releases one eye 3.8 ms before xrEndFrame and
    stuttered in it (issue 22), which OpenXR Toolkit hid by releasing its
    own swapchains at xrEndFrame (0.07 ms). V427 fixed the same case for
    native D3D12 by moving the history capture; on the bridge the copy is
    what the runtime shows, so the whole release has to move.
    
    With capture_at_end_frame on (the default), a bridged release on the
    thread that calls xrEndFrame is held (SwapchainState::pending_bridge_
    release) and run at the top of the game's xrEndFrame: the bridge's copy,
    the history capture, the runtime's own release and the mirror's
    bookkeeping, in that order. Only on that thread, because the deferred
    copy signals and flushes the game's D3D11 immediate context; elsewhere
    the release runs as before. A pending release runs early at the next
    acquire or wait on its swapchain, so the runtime sees releases in order
    and never two waited images, and is dropped when the swapchain is
    destroyed. The Vulkan bridge is unchanged.
    
    d3d11_bridge stage 4 records each deferred release (a swapchain, b
    image, c microseconds after the game's release); the summary script
    reports them. New call-chain mode d3d11-bridge-late-paint draws into an
    eye after releasing it: 9 of 10 frames reach the runtime stale on V439,
    none on V440.
    
    ctest 87/87; the three bridge modes, D3D11 interop logging, the four
    DCS scenarios and the presenter scenarios looped eight times each. Not
    yet run in a headset.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

